cartridge "CROSSROADS/Steve Harter/1987"

' CROSSROADS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Crossroads" by Steve Harter, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette December 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2300). Made
' for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' The game: one or two players in a maze crowded with nine kinds of
' creatures. The creatures fight each other as readily as they fight you:
' each kind has a colour, and the same colour (or a friendly pair: cyan and
' green, purple and blue, orange and brown) means friends; everything else
' is a foe. Shoot creatures for points, pick up the spinning spars (each one
' a shield point, S, and 500 at the end of the level); five spars taken by
' the players end the level. Creatures eat spars too and drop them when they
' die. A creature's attack or a bullet costs a shield point; with none left
' a life (L) is lost. Points: blob 50, gunner 150, spider 300, beetle 400,
' snapper 500, crawler 600, shell 1000, skull 1100, knight 1500.
'
' From the original (orig/crossroads.dis): the eight mazes (bit tables at
' $0F65 and $1041, mirrored halves; levels 1-4 take the first four, later
' levels a random one), the random wall style and colour each level; the
' creature table at $15FE (speed, sight, hit points, points, colour/team
' and behaviour flags: flees foes, chases foes, wants spars, ignores
' bullets, herds with friends, shoots, bullets bounce off its sides,
' attacking costs it a hit point); the level table at $1138 (L1: 36
' blobs, 25 gunners, 15 spiders, 12 beetles ...) and the random mix from
' level 5 ($1158, +32 of one kind); the friend table at $11A4; the move
' choice (four directions weighted 100, +/- 40 - distance for what is seen
' first in each, +1 straight on, -1 back, two random +1s; a turn takes a
' move; walking into a foe attacks it, into a spar eats it); creature moves
' handed out in turn by per-creature speed counters, 2 a jiffy at level 1,
' one more every odd level; bullets from the cell ahead, cooldown 12
' jiffies, through anything into the first object (no friend check);
' bouncing off shells and knights unless they are hit in the face; the
' player's 3 shield points (S, at most 9), 4 lives, an extra life every
' 10000; 40 jiffies of stillness on each start; 5 spars a level plus up to
' 5 more at random; 500 a spar at the end of the level; the edges wrap
' round.
'
' Liberties: the 39 x 23 mazes are condensed to 27 x 21 for 8-pixel cells
' (mazes.py drops 6 mirrored column pairs and 2 rows, keeping every open
' cell connected); at most 30 creatures (the C64 had 72-88), the level's
' counts scaled down in proportion, and the move rate scaled with them so
' each creature keeps the C64's pace; everything runs at half the C64's
' speed (players a cell every 8 frames, bullets every 2: the C64's 15 and
' 60 cells a second were too fast on a TV); a creature moves at most every
' 16 frames on average however few are left (the C64's last creatures
' raced); the players are smooth sprites gliding between cells (the C64
' used characters); point values from the code ($15FE: 500 and 600 for
' snapper and crawler; the C64 title's list said 600 and 1000); a bullet
' fired point-blank into a bouncing creature is lost; one button fires,
' joystick only (the C64's keys to recolour a player and pause are gone);
' a high score; GAME OVER, then the title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (player 1) and controller 2 (player 2): the stick
' walks (pushing a new way first turns), a button fires. On the title, up
' and down choose 1 PLAYER, 2 PLAYERS, HOW TO PLAY or CREDITS, a button
' chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

